@<Module@/Go Structure@=
{"description": "Common structures in Go programming"}
@

gofmt reads a Go source file on stdin and writes the formatted file on stdout.
The Go toolchain fixes the format, so this filter has no style file.
@<Go Structure@/Filters@/format Go code@=#!/bin/sh
gofmt
@

@<Module@/Go Structure@/Filters@=
{"description": "Format a Go source file."}
@

@<Module@/Go Structure@/Flag@=
{"description": "Flags and positional arguments of a command."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/file@=
{"description": "One Go source file."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/module meta information@=
{"description": "Package name and other properties, as a Perl hash."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/module properties@=
{"description": "Extra Perl hash entries for the module meta information."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/package clause@=
{"description": "Package comment followed by the package clause."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/package name@=
{"description": "Package identifier from the module meta information."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/package comment@=
{"description": "Comment lines immediately above the package clause."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/build constraints@=
{"description": "go:build lines before the package clause."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/imports@=
{"description": "Import declaration, omitted when there are no paths."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/import paths@=
{"description": "Lines of the import declaration."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/constants@=
{"description": "Constant declarations."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/variables@=
{"description": "Variable declarations."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/types@=
{"description": "Type declarations."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/methods@=
{"description": "Method declarations."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/functions@=
{"description": "Function declarations."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/main body@=
{"description": "Statements of func main in a command."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Filters@/format Go code@=
{"description": "Format stdin as Go and write it to stdout."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/invocation syntax@=
{"description": "Flags and positional arguments of a command."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/flag syntax helpers@=
{"description": "Perl subroutines shared by the flag generators."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/command variables@=
{"description": "Variable command, when the command has flags or arguments."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/register flags@=
{"description": "flag.Var calls for the invocation syntax."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/usage literal@=
{"description": "Go string literal of the usage line."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/store arguments@=
{"description": "Statements that check and store positional arguments."}
@

@<Chunk@/Go Structure@/Flag@/parse command line arguments@=
{"description": "Register flags, parse the command line, and store arguments."}
@

A Go source file is one tree.  Build constraints come first,
because the go command reads them only before the package clause.
The package clause is next, then the import declaration,
then constants, variables, types, methods, and functions.
Methods follow types, and free functions follow methods,
so a reader can list either sequence on its own.
A type that must stay beside its methods belongs in one block.
Extend a sequence by adding a block.
One package may contain several files.
A block with no label is part of every file.
Put a shared label to the right of a more specific one:
blocks labeled lib are part of a reference impl@@:lib.
A block that belongs to one file carries that file's whole label list.
The line break before the closing control of a block is not content,
so a file block may close on the line after its reference.
@<Go Structure@/file@=
@<.@/build constraints@>
@<.@/package clause@>
@<.@/imports@>
@<.@/constants@>
@<.@/variables@>
@<.@/types@>
@<.@/methods@>
@<.@/functions@>
@|Go Structure@/Filters@/format Go code@

go doc attaches the comment that ends immediately above the package clause.
Leave no blank line between that comment and the clause.
@<Go Structure@/package clause@=
@<.@/package comment@|Text Processing@/remove ending newlines@>
package @<.@/package name@>
@

@<Go Structure@/package name@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my %meta = @<.@/module meta information@>;
my $name = $meta{name};
die "package name '$name' is not a Go identifier\n"
    if $name !~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/;
print $name;
@|Basic@/evaluate@

The name entry is the package identifier.
pkg stands in until a file sets its own name.
A command does not read this name; its package clause says main.
Further entries belong in module properties.
@<Go Structure@/module meta information@=
(
    name => 'pkg',
    @<.@/module properties@>
)
@

No import declaration is emitted when the path list is empty
or contains only whitespace.
A blank line between paths starts another group,
and gofmt keeps that group.
@<Go Structure@/imports@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my $paths = <<'LITAR_END_IMPORTS';
@<.@/import paths@>
LITAR_END_IMPORTS
$paths =~ s/\A\s+//;
$paths =~ s/\s+\z//;
print "import (\n$paths\n)\n" if $paths ne '';
@|Basic@/evaluate@

command is the rightmost label of a package main program.
These blocks set the package name to main,
add the flag, fmt, and os imports,
add the command variable when the invocation syntax needs one,
and add func main.
func main parses the command line and then runs the main body.
Parsing always calls flag.Parse, flag.Usage, and os.Exit,
so those three imports stay used when the command has no flags.
Replace module meta information to name the other files in the node.
That replacement does not rename the command.
@<command@:Go Structure@/package name@=main@

@<command@:Go Structure@/import paths@+
    "flag"
    "fmt"
    "os"
@

@<command@:Go Structure@/variables@+
@<.@/Flag@/command variables@>
@

@<Go Structure@/Flag@/command variables@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my $syntax = @<.@/invocation syntax@>;
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>
my @@flags = flag_list($syntax);
my @@arguments = argument_list($syntax);
exit 0 if !@@flags && !@@arguments;
my %seen_flag;
my %seen_arg;
print "var command struct {\n";
if (@@flags) {
    print "flags struct {\n";
    for my $flag (@@flags) {
        my $field = take_field($flag->{name});
        die "name '$flag->{name}' repeats field $field\n" if $seen_flag{$field}++;
        die "unsupported flag type: $flag->{type}\n" if !flag_func($flag->{type});
        print "$field $flag->{type}\n";
    }
    print "}\n";
}
if (@@arguments) {
    print "arguments struct {\n";
    for my $arg (@@arguments) {
        my $field = take_field($arg);
        die "name '$arg' repeats field $field\n" if $seen_arg{$field}++;
        print "$field string\n";
    }
    print "}\n";
}
print "}\n";
@|Basic@/evaluate@

@<command@:Go Structure@/functions@+
func main() {
    @<.@/Flag@/parse command line arguments@>
    @<.@/main body@>
}
@

@<Go Structure@/Flag@/parse command line arguments@=
@<.@/register flags@>flag.Usage = func() {
fmt.Fprint(flag.CommandLine.Output(), @<.@/usage literal@>)
flag.PrintDefaults()
}
flag.Parse()
@<.@/store arguments@>@

@<Go Structure@/Flag@/register flags@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my $syntax = @<.@/invocation syntax@>;
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>
for my $flag (flag_list($syntax)) {
    my $field = take_field($flag->{name});
    my $func = flag_func($flag->{type});
    die "unsupported flag type: $flag->{type}\n" if !$func;
    my $lit = go_literal($flag->{type}, $flag->{default});
    my $usage = go_string($flag->{usage} // '');
    my $name = go_string($flag->{name});
    print "flag.$func(&command.flags.$field, $name, $lit, $usage)\n";
}
@|Basic@/evaluate@

@<Go Structure@/Flag@/usage literal@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my $syntax = @<.@/invocation syntax@>;
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>
my $name = $syntax->{name} // 'command';
my @@parts = ($name);
for my $flag (flag_list($syntax)) {
    my $opt = '-' . $flag->{name};
    if (($flag->{type} // '') ne 'bool') {
        my $meta = uc($flag->{name} // '');
        $meta =~ tr/- /_/;
        $opt .= " $meta";
    }
    push @@parts, "[$opt]";
}
for my $arg (argument_list($syntax)) {
    my $shown = uc $arg;
    $shown =~ tr/- /_/;
    push @@parts, $shown;
}
print go_string('Usage: ' . join(' ', @@parts) . "\n");
@|Basic@/evaluate@

@<Go Structure@/Flag@/store arguments@=
@<Text Processing@/basic perl settings@>
my $syntax = @<.@/invocation syntax@>;
@<.@/flag syntax helpers@>
my @@arguments = argument_list($syntax);
my $n = @@arguments;
print "if len(flag.Args()) != $n {\n";
print "flag.Usage()\n";
print "os.Exit(1)\n";
print "}\n";
my $i = 0;
for my $arg (@@arguments) {
    my $field = take_field($arg);
    print "command.arguments.$field = flag.Args()[$i]\n";
    $i++;
}
@|Basic@/evaluate@

A command line is one Perl hash.
The filters below turn that hash into Go,
and the program uses the standard flag package.
name is the word in the usage line.
Each flag has a name, a flag package type, a default, and a usage string.
The type is bool, string, int, int64, uint, uint64, or float64.
A bool default is a Perl boolean.
A string default is a Perl string.
A numeric default is a Perl number.
The Go field is the flag name in camel case.
Hyphens and spaces separate words.
Positional arguments are an exact list.
An omitted list is empty,
so the command accepts no positional arguments.
flag.Usage prints the usage line and the flag defaults.
A man page is not generated.

{
    name => COMMAND_NAME,
    flags => [
        {
            name => FLAG_NAME,
            type => TYPE,
            default => DEFAULT,
            usage => USAGE,
        },
    ],
    arguments => [
        ARGUMENT,
    ],
}
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/invocation syntax@=
{}
@

The generators include this chunk as one fragment,
so a flag name becomes the same Go field in each of them.
@<Go Structure@/Flag@/flag syntax helpers@=
my %go_keyword = map { $_ => 1 } qw(
    break default func interface select
    case defer go map struct
    chan else goto package switch
    const fallthrough if range type
    continue for import return var
);

sub flag_list {
    my ($syntax) = @@_;
    return () if !$syntax->{flags};
    return @@{$syntax->{flags}};
}

sub argument_list {
    my ($syntax) = @@_;
    return () if !$syntax->{arguments};
    return @@{$syntax->{arguments}};
}

sub field_name {
    my ($name) = @@_;
    $name =~ s/\s+/-/g;
    my @@parts = grep { length } split /[-_]+/, $name;
    return '' if !@@parts;
    my $out = lc shift @@parts;
    $out .= ucfirst lc for @@parts;
    return $out;
}

sub take_field {
    my ($name) = @@_;
    my $field = field_name($name);
    die "name '$name' is not a Go field\n"
        if $field !~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/;
    die "name '$name' is a Go keyword\n" if $go_keyword{$field};
    return $field;
}

sub go_string {
    my ($s) = @@_;
    $s //= '';
    $s =~ s/\\/\\\\/g;
    $s =~ s/"/\\"/g;
    $s =~ s/\n/\\n/g;
    $s =~ s/\r/\\r/g;
    $s =~ s/\t/\\t/g;
    return "\"$s\"";
}

sub go_literal {
    my ($type, $default) = @@_;
    if (!defined $default) {
        return 'false' if $type eq 'bool';
        return '""' if $type eq 'string';
        return '0';
    }
    return $default ? 'true' : 'false' if $type eq 'bool';
    return go_string($default) if $type eq 'string';
    return $default;
}

sub flag_func {
    my ($type) = @@_;
    my %func = (
        bool => 'BoolVar',
        string => 'StringVar',
        int => 'IntVar',
        int64 => 'Int64Var',
        uint => 'UintVar',
        uint64 => 'Uint64Var',
        float64 => 'Float64Var',
    );
    return $func{$type};
}
@
